'''Open-source software''' is software distributed under a license that permits users to inspect, use, modify and redistribute its source code under defined conditions.

== Licensing and governance ==
The Open Source Initiative's Open Source Definition requires more than source availability, including redistribution and permission for derived works without discrimination against persons or fields of endeavor. Permissive licenses such as MIT and Apache-2.0 generally allow broad reuse subject to conditions; copyleft licenses such as GPLv3 impose reciprocal conditions on certain distributions of covered derivatives. Exact obligations depend on the license and how software is combined and distributed; do not infer them solely from the project label.

== Engineering lifecycle ==
Open repositories can support transparent issue tracking, code review, reproducible builds and public release history. A project may still accept contributions only after maintainer review. Separate licensing from governance, release integrity and dependency security. An openly licensed dependency can contain vulnerabilities or unreviewed changes; source availability creates an opportunity for inspection, not proof that inspection occurred.

== Evaluation and adoption ==
Evaluate release provenance, tests, maintainer responsiveness, supported versions, dependency licenses and security disclosures. Reproducible builds can compare artifacts from independent build environments. Assess sustainability through maintenance capacity rather than stars alone. Open-source software differs from open data, open access and open standards, which concern different artifacts and rights.

